 ],
 "literal": "Not considering youth, desiring wealth, he who went / even here has not returned, where is he? saying / nurtured by the rain, the flowering creeper mullai's / clustered buds as shining teeth, / smiles indeed, O friend, the fragrant cool rainy season!",
 "translation": "Not considering our youth, desiring wealth, he departed. He has not returned here yet; where could he be? Mocking my plight, O friend, the fragrant, cool rainy season smiles at me, showing the clustered buds of the rain-nurtured mullai creeper as its shining teeth.",
 "modern": "நம் இளமையைப் பொருட்படுத்தாமல், செல்வத்தை விரும்பிச் சென்றவர் இன்னும் இங்கு வரவில்லை; அவர் எங்கே இருக்கிறாரோ? தோழியே! மழையால் வளர்க்கப்பட்ட பூங்கொடியான முல்லையின் திரண்ட மொட்டுகளைத் தன் விளங்கும் பற்களாகக் கொண்டு, இந்த நறிய குளிர்ந்த கார்காலம் நம்மைப் பார்த்துப் புன்னகைக்கிறது.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend in distress. The hero had promised to return before the onset of the rainy season, but he has not yet arrived. The rainy season has now commenced, bringing fresh showers that nurture the mullai jasmine creepers. The white, clustered buds of the jasmine look like a row of shining teeth. To the lonely heroine, this sight feels as if the cool, fragrant rainy season is mocking her waiting state with a bright, white smile. This poem belongs to the Mullai tiṇai, where the forest landscape, the rainy season, and the mullai flower represent the theme of patient waiting.",
